  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Tuesday/...An active weather \npattern will persist across SEAK through the forecast period. \nMultiple fronts/lows will push through the Gulf move into the \npanhandle over the weekend and into early next week. The big \nplayer that will send the fronts into the Gulf and through SEAK \nwill be the remnants of Typhoon Dujuan and associated upper- level\nlow/trough that will slowly swing through southern interior into \nthe Gulf through the weekend into early next week. Between each \nfront, weaker moist onshore flow will keep mostly cloudy skies and\nshowers. The initial front will lift northeastward through SEAK \non Saturday, followed quickly by a secondary stronger front \npushing east later Saturday night and Sunday. A period of moderate\nto heavy rain and gusty S-SEly winds are expected with each \nfrontal passage, especially with the Sunday system as deeper \nmoisture feed ahead of front will bring higher precipitation \nrates. Cooler temperatures aloft with the upper trough will \ngenerate modest instability, bringing a potential for isolated \nthunderstorms, mainly along the Gulf coast. By early next week, \nguidance continues to indicate the active pattern continuing as \nthe broad upper-level troughing remains over or just west of the \nGulf, with an additional front/low lifting northeast through the \nGulf into the Panhandle Monday into Tuesday, keeping the potential\nfor moderate to heavy rain and gusty winds. Some guidance is \nquite aggressive with this low and bringing gales into the Gulf. \nWhile model spread and uncertainly remains in regards to the \ntiming, location and strength of this system, overall confidence \nis high for a wet and active period. While rounds of moderate to \nheavy rainfall are expected through the period, with around a \n10-15% probability of precip rates reaching 6hr/24hr 2-year return\ninterval, each system is fairly progressive and a prolonged \nduration of heavy rain is not expected. The rainy and cloudy \npattern will keep temperatures seasonably cool with highs \ngenerally in the 50s.",
